  "response": "Short version: the payment test only checks that `api.post` was called with `\"/charges\"`. **None** of its collaborators are real. Most changes to how payments are submitted, including ones that charge customers wrongly, would leave it green.\n\n## What the test asserts\n\n`src/services/payments.test.ts:10-14` has a single case, `\"submitPayment › posts the charge\"`:\n\n```ts\nawait submitPayment({ total: 100 });\nexpect(api.post).toHaveBeenCalled();\nexpect(api.post).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"/charges\", expect.anything());\n```\n\nBoth assertions only look at how the mock was called (scan finding `mock_saturation`, `crime_00018`: 2 mock-interaction assertions, 0 behavioural). The test never checks:\n- what `submitPayment` returns\n- the `amount` or `timeout` it sends (the second argument is `expect.anything()`)\n- how many times it posts (it uses `toHaveBeenCalled`, not `toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)`)\n- what happens on failure (the mock always resolves, so the retry loop never runs)\n\n## How much is real\n\n- **Collaborators:** 0 of 2 are real (100% mocked; the subject itself isn't counted). `../lib/api.js` and `../lib/stripe.js` are both replaced by `vi.fn()` stubs with no implementation. Those two cover the external-request boundary and the payment boundary.\n- **Only real code:** `submitPayment` itself.\n- **`refundPayment` has no test at all**, even though it wraps `stripe.refunds.create` in a 5-attempt retry with no idempotency key (`crime_00014`).\n\n`crimes context` lists this as the only likely test and puts the file's test-gap in the bottom quartile. With mocks this thin, that ranking is misleading.\n\n## One change that breaks production but keeps the test green\n\nRemove the `return` on `src/services/payments.ts:14`:\n\n```ts\nawait api.post(\"/charges\", { amount: order.total, timeout: timeoutSeconds });\n```\n\nThe loop no longer exits after a successful charge, so every payment posts to `/charges` **three times** and the customer is charged three times. `submitPayment` also returns `undefined` and logs \"payment failed\". The test still passes: `toHaveBeenCalled` and `toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"/charges\", anything)` hold whether there are 1 or 3 calls.\n\nTwo other changes the test can't see: sending `amount: order.total * 100` (a unit mistake), or dropping `amount` entirely.\n\n## Other things to know before you change this code\n\n- **The test may not even run as written.** I read the files but didn't run the test. The mock factory returns `{ post, get }`, but the test imports a named `{ api }`, so `api` would be undefined in the test. `payments.ts` never imports `api` or `stripe`; they're used as globals. And `src/lib/` doesn't exist in this checkout. So \"green\" here may mean \"never executed\".\n- **The retry problem is already there** (`crime_00015`). It replays a POST with no idempotency key, retries every error, and has no backoff.\n- **The timeout is likely wrong.** It's read straight from `process.env.REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`, defaults to `\"30\"`, and is named `timeoutSeconds`. `src/config/index.ts` defaults the same variable to `5000` (`crime_00011`). The test can't catch this because the timeout falls under `expect.anything()`.\n\nBefore you change submission, I'd add tests that check results: `toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)` on success, the exact payload (`{ amount: 100, timeout: … }`), the returned value, and a case where the first attempt rejects. That last one should check that every retry sends the same idempotency key.",
